  "worked_solution": "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (0,1)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(c) & =\\frac{f(1)-f(0)}{1-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{3}-{0}^{3}}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(c)=3{c}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{c}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {c}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ c & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c\\in (0,1)$, we discard the negative value, therefore $c=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$",
